<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ionians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ionians were one of the main groups of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, speaking the Ionic dialect and living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and, from about 1000 BCE, the central west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>. Their twelve cities there, including Miletus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Chios, formed the Ionian League. Ionia was the birthplace of Greek philosophy and science.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ionians.jpg" alt="Ionians" width="320" height="552" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Davey · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xerxes_I_tomb_Ionian_soldier_circa_470_BCE_cleaned_up.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ionian Greeks</b> settled the island around 1000 BCE, and Chios became one of the twelve members of the Ionian League, which met at the Panionion on Mount Mycale.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Above the city stood the Amphictyonic League, or Amphictyony, an ancient association of twelve peoples, mostly of central and northern Greece, among them the Thessalians, Boeotians, Phocians, Locrians, Dorians and <b>Ionians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the collapse of the Bronze Age world, <b>Ionian Greeks</b> settled Miletus around 1000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek tradition held that the island had earlier been settled by Carians and Leleges and that Ionian colonists arrived under a leader named Procles from Epidaurus; whatever the details, <b>Ionian Greeks</b> were established on Samos by about 1000 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Twelve peoples, among them the Thessalians, Phocians, Boeotians, Dorians and <b>Ionians</b>, each sent two delegates (hieromnemones) to a council meeting twice a year at Anthela and Delphi.</blockquote><a href="/state/amphictyonic-league">Amphictyonic League</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 6th century BCE Bias of Priene, counted among the Seven Sages, advised the <b>Ionians</b> to emigrate to Sardinia rather than submit to Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/priene">Priene</a></li></ul></section>
